Saw a TV program on Edelman's Neural Darwinism last night.  They showed
the Darwin IV (NOMAD) automaton in action.  It would take about 2 - 3
hours of training to get from the "newborn baby" stage to being able to
track light.  Later, it was taught to associate color with "taste".

The "taste" test involved checking blocks for magnetism, it would select
one type and reject the other.  But eventually it would learn that the
desired type were a particular color, and would select blocks solely on
the basis of color, dispensing with the magnetic tests.

(Someone said that Gerald Edelman would be equivalent to the Darwin XXIX
model.)

By the way, I like Reilly's conceptual field model (with consensual 
beliefs being like gravity wells, and individual beliefs like marbles
rolling along the space).  Another risk (apart from losing your marbles)
if your beliefs never settle into any attractors, even temporarily, is
that the whole conceptual space starts to represent the edge of a razor
blade, with your brain like a marble or pea rolling along it.  ("One's"
/ "my" to be substituted as deemed appropriate!)

