From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 1994 09:03:24 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-498] Pancritical Rationalism

A brief summary of PCR (mixed with a few other tidbits):				

1) things don't need to be justified (in terms of axioms) to be
believed.  Instead, use a system of rationality such that you can
start with whatever beliefs you currently have an throw out
inconsistent ones.

2) beliefs are never true or false, just more or less credible (this
is the generalization on Popper).  Thus, "falsifying" observations
reduce the credibility of a belief (presumably by a lot).  In
addition, heuristics can reduce the credibility of ideas by a lot
("Rifkin said it, so it's probably bogus.")  This makes for a much
richer toolset than mere falsification, better models how we actually
manage beliefs, and allows rationalis to apply to non-objective things
like ethics (there are more and less effective sets of ethics, for
example, even though falsification in that domain is tough).  When
doing things in the world, act on the basis of the beliefs with the
most credibility.

2) magic thinking *explains* the world.  Rationality attempts to
predict it.  "Thor is pissed," explains why a thunder storm just
happened, but doesn't help predict when other storms will happen.
"Dampness pisses Thor off," has better predictive power :-)  Thus,
statements that don't make a prediction (or cannot be cast so as to
make a prediction) have little rational content ("All particles move
because they are carried by small blue rabbits that are impossible to
see" --disprove it), and should default to having little credit (thus
most religion is bunk :-).

3) survival is an arational goal.  PCR claims that being able to
predict the world improves chances of survival.  If this claim doesn't
seem to be true, discredit it and stop acting according to it.

4) Hold all beliefs tentatively--nothing is true, only very credible.
The scientific rule of repeatability (science is based on hypotheses
that remain true even after many experiments) is merely very credible,
and would remain a useful tool even if it didn't always work
("occasionally, the rules of physics change").  PCR itself is only
credible (according to itself) as long as it continues to bea system
for managing beliefs that increase ones ability to operate in the
observable universe. This last point isthe "Pan" part of it: it
applies to itself (and it's not justificational), so nothing need be
taken on faith.

Comments, you other PCR folk?

dean

