From: peb@procase.com (Paul Baclace)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 94 14:10:59 PST
Subject: [#94-1-515] Safe Cars

Politics aside, here are some observations about car safety (an important
subject given that it is the most likely cause of death, aside from AIDS,
in the 20-40 year old age bracket):

In a radio interview with a pathologist (who was featured on an HBO
special (hence the radio promo)), mentioned something about car safety
a few weeks ago.   He said the major improvements in car safety
resulted in studying the corpses and determining that secondary
injuries--not the primary impact--were the main cause of death.  The
introduction of safety glass that shatters into tiny pieces instead of
making large blades for bodies to impact upon had made a noticable
reduction in the death rate.

Another reduction was caused by eliminating sharp objects like metal
switches on the dash board for a head to land on.  Older cars had
more of these while newer cars have smaller and breakable protrusions
on the dashboard.  I'm sure that steering wheels became breakable
over the years too.

On a primary impact note: 

Somewhere else I heard that bigger, not heavier cars are safer.  MV^2
says that mass is important, but during impact a large car has more
linear crumple space to lower the massive deceleration.  Because of
this, I'd like to have a car that gets bigger with speed, but has a
compact size for parking in the city...


