>From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)

>>I actively set up my environment as a memory trigger to make this possible.
>>It takes time to keep it all organized.
>
>>Care to tell how?

I use reminders like notes, books, drawings, pictures, etc. around my offices.
It appears to the casual observer to be messy, but actually, it is a widely
branching hierarchy rather than a narrow and deep hierarchy.  The messy
desk is a constant reminder of things I want to track and do.  When 
searching for things I'm apt to run into something else and that can trigger
ideas and prompt other action.  This is like Brian Eno's Oblique Strategy
method (see The Vertical Color of Sound, a thesis on Eno) for creativity.


