Here's a combination that addresses the issues in the above paragraphs:
(1) Advanced fusion as a power source (i.e. not Tokamaks, which represent
primitive fusion).  Either Paul Koloc's plasmak concept or the electric
confinement concept developed by Bussard and being tested by Univ. of Ill.
would be sufficient.
(2) Use lots of power to process any input (landfills, nuclear waste dumps)
through a mass spectrometer, separating it into constituent elements and
isotopes.  Actually a set of mass spectrometers and some chemical separation
would be needed (there are different elements with the same atomic weight
from different isotopes).  You end up with pure materials to go and do
whatever you want with.  

> I care about Earth's future because it is necessary that I live on it
> until I can find a way to do otherwise.  Also, given the way we have evolved, 
> much of what we consider "happiness" is triggered by experiences 
> intimately tied with the Earth, so I'd imagine we will want to maintain 
> connection with it -- especially when we can.
> 

You can leave the Earth if you want to.  The cost is about $15 million per
year (yes million, not billion), if you do it correctly.  Here's how:

(1) Develop a propulsion module consisting of one RL-10 engine, tanks,
and brains (four of these engines are used on the DC-X).  Start flying
with a solid kick motor on top, delivering a couple of hundred lb per
launch.  Propulsion module is recoverable and reused from sub-orbital
flights. 

(2) Deliver some inflatable work bubbles, food, air, etc. using the
small rocket.

(3) Get a guy up there using the Shuttle or Soyuz, leave in orbit.

(4) Launch spools of cable with small rocket, and continuing stream
of food and air to keep worker alive.

(5) Worker assembles hanging orbital tether from spools of cable,
lowering velocity of bottom end.

(6) Payload of small rocket increases as it now flies to bottom
of tether rather than all the way to orbit.  In a couple of dozen
flights, you don't need the solid kick motor any longer, just the
propulsion module, which now delivers about 2000 lb per launch.

(7) Additional crew can be delivered using the 2000 lb payload
capacity.

(8) You pay for all this by underselling other rocket launchers
delivering things like the Iridium constellation.  Or you just do it
for the hell of it if you are rich enough (If you have a couple
of hundred million in worth, you could fund this out of your pocket)

